  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nMain focus over the next couple of days will be flattening of upper \nlevel ridge in response to an approaching, somewhat deamplifying \nwave. Signals past several days have been a line of showers and \nstorms forming on the sfc front to the NW Tuesday afternoon and \nprogressing east Tuesday night, most likely weakening as it arrives \nin our NW counties towards Wed AM, with potential for renewed \ndevelopment in the afternoon in the SE half or so of the area. \nMoisture (Dewpoints upper 60s to maybe low 70s), decent instability \n(SBCAPE 1000-2000 J/KG) and at least minimally favorable shear \n(around 30 kts) will exist Wed afternoon/eve, resulting in SPC \nintroducing a Marginal Risk of severe storms in SE areas. That being \nsaid, several models have sort of backed off on coverage Tuesday \nnight and also location of redevelopment Wed afternoon/eve. Cloud \ncover Wednesday will play a huge role in what unfolds across our \narea both with convective potential and eventual afternoon highs. \nGiven the items noted, it is hard to buy into model blend of \ncategorical pops far NW Wed AM and SE Wed night with previous shift \ncapping pops in the likely range (more reasonable but possibly still \noverdone). Did cap pops Wed AM to likely, but for now will leave the \nsmall sliver of cat pops in my far SE Wed night. \n\nThe initial front/pre-frontal trough will weaken with time Wed \nnight into Thur and stall or wash out with another disturbance \narriving later Thu into Thu night to finally push out the lingering \nlow level moisture and allow a return to more seasonable \ntemperatures as the west to east elongated upper ridge is suppressed \nacross the southern half of the US through the weekend. A deeper \ntrough enters the central US for the start of the work week which \nmay bring chances for showers and storms as well as warmer\ntemps.",
